---
title: Cloudflare (Edge, Advanced)
id: cloudflare
order: 11
description: "Run a coding-agent harness and a live preview at the edge with @tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare — Workers, Containers, and Durable Objects in one deploy."
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want your coding agent to run "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"at the edge"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — UI, agent loop, and the\nsandbox container all in one Cloudflare Worker — and to hand users a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"live\npreview URL"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of whatever the agent builds. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare"},{"type":"text","value":"\nprovides the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cloudflareSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" provider plus a ready-made agent coordinator so\nyou don't hand-roll the Worker ↔ container plumbing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This page covers the edge-specific concerns. For the provider-agnostic basics\n(workspace, tools, policy, lifecycle) start at the "},{"type":"link","href":"./overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overview"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"two-execution-models","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two execution models"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where the harness loop and its "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tool bridge"}]},{"type":"text","value":" run is a deployment\nchoice. The Cloudflare layer supports two shapes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"do-drives-container-default","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DO-drives-container (default)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The orchestrator (a Durable Object) runs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" and the tool-bridge; the\ncontainer only runs the agent CLI. The bridge is served from the orchestrator's\nown "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" handler — no raw TCP listener — and the agent reaches it across the\ncontainer → orchestrator boundary, so the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"whole MCP protocol"}]},{"type":"text","value":" crosses that\nboundary. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"examples/sandbox-cloudflare"},{"type":"text","value":" TanStack Start app demonstrates this:\nUI, agent, Durable Objects, and the container in one Worker."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"co-located-in-container","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Co-located (in-container)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The harness loop "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"and"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the tool-bridge run inside the container — the\nin-container sandbox is just "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localProcessSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":", with native stdin and a\nlocalhost "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"node:http"},{"type":"text","value":" bridge. The only thing that still crosses back to the\norchestrator is host "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tool execution"}]},{"type":"text","value":": a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" tool's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" closure\n(your DB, secrets, app state) lives on the orchestrator, not in the container.\nThe public surface shrinks from the whole MCP protocol to a single authenticated\ntool-exec call."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enable it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCloudflareSandboxAgent({ mode: 'colocated' })"},{"type":"text","value":" plus a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runInContainerHarness"},{"type":"text","value":" container program from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/runner"},{"type":"text","value":". The seam is four exports from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox"},{"type":"text","value":": the orchestrator serializes its tools with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDescriptors(tools)"},{"type":"text","value":" and ships the descriptors in; the container rebuilds\nthem with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remoteToolStubs(descriptors, executor)"},{"type":"text","value":", where each stub's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndelegates to a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RemoteToolExecutor"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"httpRemoteToolExecutor(url, token)"},{"type":"text","value":" POSTs\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ name, args }"},{"type":"text","value":" back); the orchestrator answers that one call with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executeHostTool(tools, name, args)"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport {\n  defineSandbox,\n  defineWorkspace,\n  httpRemoteToolExecutor,\n  remoteToolStubs,\n  withSandbox,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\nimport { request } from './run-request'\n\n// Inside the container: the orchestrator POSTed `{ messages, toolDescriptors,\n// toolExecUrl, toolExecToken }`. Rebuild its tools as stubs whose execute()\n// POSTs back; the adapter bridges them over the in-container localhost MCP\n// transport, and only that one tool-exec call leaves the container.\nchat({\n  threadId: request.threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages: request.messages,\n  tools: remoteToolStubs(\n    request.toolDescriptors,\n    httpRemoteToolExecutor(request.toolExecUrl, request.toolExecToken),\n  ),\n  // The in-container sandbox is just local-process (native stdin + a localhost\n  // node:http bridge).\n  middleware: [\n    withSandbox(\n      defineSandbox({\n        id: 'in-container',\n        provider: localProcessSandbox(),\n        workspace: defineWorkspace({ source: { type: 'none' } }),\n      }),\n    ),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"durable-runs-at-the-edge","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable runs at the edge"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare is the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"log-first tier"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of the portable durable-runs protocol —\nnot a separate architecture. Read\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./durable-runs#the-two-tiers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Runs Explained"}]},{"type":"text","value":" first; this section is\nwhat the edge changes and what it deliberately does not."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The coordinator Durable Object owns each run and persists every emitted chunk\ninto a DO-storage-backed run log ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DurableObjectRunEventLog"},{"type":"text","value":") under a monotonic\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"seq"},{"type":"text","value":". Clients only ever tail that log from a cursor, so a refresh, a dropped\nWebSocket, or a coordinator that hibernated between chunks reconnects "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"without\never touching the sandbox"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"link","href":"./journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":" keeps its portable job on\nthe driver side — warm reattach and the driver's resume position — and the\nprecedence rule is the portable one: the log wins for what clients see, the\njournal wins for where the driver resumes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What is genuinely Cloudflare-specific is the DO's "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"supervised lifetime"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the\ndriver outlives any request and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alarm()"},{"type":"text","value":" gives it a scheduler. Off Cloudflare,\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./takeover","children":[{"type":"text","value":"takeover"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the substitute for exactly that, which is why the\njournal stays mandatory on every tier."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"One portable protocol\" is literal here, not a framing: the coordinator drives\nruns with "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"core's run driver"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunController"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox"},{"type":"text","value":"),\nbound to the DO log by two adapters — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runLogStore"},{"type":"text","value":" exposes the log as core's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runLogStream"},{"type":"text","value":" exposes one run of it as core's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":". Both are exported from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent"},{"type":"text","value":" for apps composing directly, and they\nare what lets the portable pieces ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alignToStoredLog"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"replayRunStream"},{"type":"text","value":") work\nagainst the DO log the same way they do against "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The vocabulary is core's throughout: statuses are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"},{"type":"text","value":" /\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aborted"},{"type":"text","value":", and the record is core's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunRecord"},{"type":"text","value":" plus the log's own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastSeq"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncursor ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunLogRecord"},{"type":"text","value":"). Records a Durable Object persisted under the pre-1.0\nlayout ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"done"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" statuses, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createdAt"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"updatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" fields) are\nmigrated in place on first read — nothing to run, but note that\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET /runs/:id"},{"type":"text","value":" and the WebSocket terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" frame now carry the\nconverged status strings and field names."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"stall-watchdog","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stall watchdog"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCloudflareSandboxAgent"},{"type":"text","value":" sets one stall policy for the whole app. The\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stallTimeoutMs"},{"type":"text","value":" field applies to every run in both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"do-drives"},{"type":"text","value":" (the default)\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colocated"},{"type":"text","value":" mode:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Omitted"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Treat the run as stalled after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"300000"},{"type":"text","value":" ms (five minutes) without persisted activity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Positive safe integer"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use that many milliseconds as the stall threshold"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable stall detection"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"import { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { createCloudflareSandboxAgent } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\n\nexport const agent = createCloudflareSandboxAgent({\n  adapter: () => grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  stallTimeoutMs: 10 * 60_000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The alarm checks every 30 seconds, or every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stallTimeoutMs"},{"type":"text","value":" when that is\nshorter, so detection can lag the configured threshold by up to one check\ninterval. Persisted run events refresh\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"updatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":"; authenticated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/_bridge"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/tool-exec"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks also refresh it\non arrival and completion. Unknown-run and unauthorized requests do not."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose a timeout longer than the longest legitimate quiet period. Native\noperations that emit no event and make no callback are invisible to the\nwatchdog. An authenticated callback is protected while it remains in flight.\nA callback that never completes can leave an orphaned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"running"},{"type":"text","value":" record.\nDisabling the watchdog with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" can do the same. When a stall is detected, the watchdog marks\nthe run log "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"},{"type":"text","value":"; it may not kill the underlying agent process or container.\nUse provider lifecycle controls and "},{"type":"link","href":"./reaping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reaping"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for resource cleanup."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"three-layers-three-homes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three layers, three homes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep these separate — each has a different home on Cloudflare, and conflating\nthem is how workspace bytes end up \"durably\" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/tmp"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where it lives on Cloudflare"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Covered in"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run + events"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The coordinator DO's run log (DO storage)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Run Journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./takeover","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workspace (the files the agent edits)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The container filesystem — "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not durable"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; re-provision it on create"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./workspace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workspace"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./provisioning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provisioning"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Artifacts (outputs worth keeping)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2, via a persistence "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobStore"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ArtifactStore"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/keep-generated-files","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep generated files"}]}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-edge-durability-does-not-buy-you","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What edge durability does not buy you"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The journal is not durable here."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cloudflareSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" declares\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableFilesystem: false"},{"type":"text","value":": the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/tmp"},{"type":"text","value":" journal lives exactly as long as the\ncontainer instance. That is fine — on this tier the DO log is the durable\ncopy and the journal is driver recovery — but it means the journal must never\nbe treated as the store of record."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refresh safety is not sleep survival."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The log makes "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reconnecting\nclients"}]},{"type":"text","value":" safe. It does not keep the container awake: if the container stops,\nthe agent process dies mid-run like anywhere else, and what the log preserves\nis everything emitted up to that point."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sleepAfter"},{"type":"text","value":" footgun."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The Sandbox container class carries a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sleepAfter"},{"type":"text","value":" idle timeout. A run that is "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"detached but quiet"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — nobody\nconnected, the agent thinking or waiting — can look idle, and an idle\ncontainer sleeps. The symptom is a run that \"died for no reason\" while the\nlog dutifully preserved its prefix, which reads as durability failing when it\nis the container lifecycle working as configured. Size "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sleepAfter"},{"type":"text","value":" well\nabove your longest expected quiet stretch before relying on detached runs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Name the sandbox by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Prefer\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineSandbox({ id: input.threadId, … })"},{"type":"text","value":" (as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"examples/sandbox-cloudflare"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndoes) over a fixed id: reconnects, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exposePreview"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reuse: 'thread'"},{"type":"text","value":"\nthen all address the same container even across DO eviction."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"disconnect-detaches-stop-cancels","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disconnect detaches; Stop cancels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The portable rule holds on this path: a closed tab or dropped WebSocket only\ndetaches the tail — the DO keeps driving and the log keeps filling. Stopping is\nan explicit cancel, and on this provider that has teeth: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"killableProcesses"},{"type":"text","value":" is\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" (no signal crosses the Workers RPC boundary), so the only cancel that\nactually stops the agent is destroying the container. Aborting the run so the\nlog is marked "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aborted"},{"type":"text","value":", without the destroy, leaves the agent running — and\nbilling — behind a UI that says stopped. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./takeover#what-cancel-means-on-a-provider-that-cannot-kill","children":[{"type":"text","value":"what cancel means on a provider that cannot kill"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"callback-hosts-bridge-vs-preview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callback hosts: bridge vs preview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In both models the container is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"off-isolate compute"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — it can't use a service\nbinding or an in-process call to reach the Worker, only the network. So the\ncontainer's callback URLs need real hosts. There are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"two distinct surfaces"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nwith different reachers and therefore different correct values, resolved by\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveBridgeOrigin"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolvePreviewHost"},{"type":"text","value":" (both from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge / tool-exec"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (container → Worker: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/_bridge"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/tool-exec"},{"type":"text","value":"). Just\nneeds to "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reach"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the Worker. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PUBLIC_HOSTNAME"},{"type":"text","value":" is optional — when unset, the\nhost is derived from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST /runs"},{"type":"text","value":" trigger request, so a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*.workers.dev"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndeploy works with "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"zero config"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"local dev uses\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.docker.internal"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (the Docker host gateway, over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"http"},{"type":"text","value":") — no tunnel."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request-derivation is safe "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"on Cloudflare"}]},{"type":"text","value":", where it would be unsafe on a\ngeneric Node server: the edge dispatches a request to your Worker only when\nits hostname matches a route you own, so the request "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Host"},{"type":"text","value":" is always one of\nyour own hostnames — never attacker-chosen — and the per-run bearer token\nthat rides the URL can't be steered off-domain. On plain Node the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Host"},{"type":"text","value":"\nheader is attacker-controlled, which is why request-derivation there would be\na token-exfil / SSRF vector. (See "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the non-edge bridge.)"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preview"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (browser → Worker → container: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exposePort"},{"type":"text","value":"). Needs "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"wildcard\nDNS"}]},{"type":"text","value":", so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PREVIEW_HOSTNAME"},{"type":"text","value":" is a "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"separate"}]},{"type":"text","value":" knob. "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local"}]},{"type":"text","value":" uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*.localhost"},{"type":"text","value":"\n(browsers resolve it to loopback with zero setup — previews work locally with\nno tunnel). "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deployed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" needs a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"custom domain"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*.<domain>"},{"type":"text","value":" route:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*.workers.dev"},{"type":"text","value":" has no wildcard subdomains, so the SDK's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exposePort"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects\nit and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolvePreviewHost"},{"type":"text","value":" throws a clear error pointing at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PREVIEW_HOSTNAME"},{"type":"text","value":"\ninstead of failing deep in a run."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"exposing-a-live-preview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exposing a live preview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The package ships the browser-preview wiring so you don't hand-roll it — both\nexported from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exposePreviewTool(input, env)"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — a ready-made "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" server tool (the\nagent sees it as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exposePreview"},{"type":"text","value":"). It addresses the run's container by\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" and opens a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare quick tunnel"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to the dev server's port\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandbox.tunnels.get(port)"},{"type":"text","value":"), returning a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"https://<name>.trycloudflare.com"},{"type":"text","value":" URL."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PREVIEW_GUIDANCE"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — a system prompt that tells the agent how to start a\ndev server whose tunnel preview works. App-agnostic on purpose."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The factory is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"harness-agnostic about auth"}]},{"type":"text","value":": it binds no API key of its own.\nYour app declares the key its harness needs ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"XAI_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" for Grok Build,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" for Claude Code, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CODEX_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" for Codex, …) on its own env\ntype and supplies it as a "},{"type":"link","href":"./provisioning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace secret"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — the coordinator\ninjects each declared secret into the sandbox env by name."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  PREVIEW_GUIDANCE,\n  createCloudflareSandboxAgent,\n  exposePreviewTool,\n  resolvePreviewHost,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\nimport { cloudflareSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare'\nimport { createSecrets, defineSandbox, defineWorkspace } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport type { SandboxAgentEnv } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\n\n// Extend the package's harness-agnostic env with the key YOUR harness needs.\ninterface AppEnv extends SandboxAgentEnv {\n  XAI_API_KEY: string\n}\n\nexport const agent = createCloudflareSandboxAgent<AppEnv>({\n  adapter: () => grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  systemPrompts: [PREVIEW_GUIDANCE],\n  tools: (input, env) => [exposePreviewTool(input, env)],\n  // Supply the harness's auth here — the package binds no key. The `sandbox`\n  // resolver receives the Worker `env` per run, so the secret VALUE is read from\n  // it. A different harness declares its own, e.g. `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for Claude\n  // Code or `CODEX_API_KEY` for Codex.\n  sandbox: (input, env) =>\n    defineSandbox({\n      id: 'cf-edge-agent',\n      provider: cloudflareSandbox({\n        binding: env.Sandbox,\n        previewHostname: resolvePreviewHost(env, input),\n      }),\n      workspace: defineWorkspace({\n        source: { type: 'none' },\n        secrets: createSecrets({ XAI_API_KEY: env.XAI_API_KEY }),\n      }),\n      lifecycle: { reuse: 'thread' },\n    }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runnable example lives at\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/ai/tree/main/examples/sandbox-cloudflare","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"examples/sandbox-cloudflare"}]},{"type":"text","value":":\none app that runs Claude Code, Codex, or Grok Build — pick the harness in the\nUI (or via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HARNESS"},{"type":"text","value":" var). Same edge topology, different adapter + key."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"why-a-quick-tunnel-not-exposeport","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why a quick tunnel, not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exposePort"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exposePort"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"proxyToSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" routes the preview through the Worker's own\norigin. In local dev that origin is your Vite dev server, and Vite's middleware\nthen serves the preview's module/asset requests ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/@vite/client"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/src/*"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/@fs/*"},{"type":"text","value":") from your "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"host"}]},{"type":"text","value":" instead of the container — the page loads the wrong\ncode and breaks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A quick tunnel is served by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cloudflared"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"inside"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the sandbox ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cloudflared"},{"type":"text","value":"\nships in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cloudflare/sandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" base image), so it bypasses the Vite port\nentirely, needs "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no custom domain"}]},{"type":"text","value":" on a deploy, and forwards WebSockets — so\nthe app's HMR works. The one requirement, which "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PREVIEW_GUIDANCE"},{"type":"text","value":" instructs, is\nthat the dev server "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"accept the tunnel hostname"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (servers reject unknown\nhosts): Vite "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server: { host: true, allowedHosts: true }"},{"type":"text","value":", webpack-dev-server\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowedHosts: 'all'"},{"type":"text","value":". ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exposePort"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolvePreviewHost"},{"type":"text","value":" remain available for\napps that want the Worker to front the request on a custom domain.)"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transport:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandbox.tunnels"},{"type":"text","value":" exists only on the SDK's "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RPC"}]},{"type":"text","value":" transport —\non the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"http"},{"type":"text","value":" it throws "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"requires the RPC transport\""}]},{"type":"text","value":". So\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cloudflareSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transport: 'rpc'"},{"type":"text","value":" (and the example also sets\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SANDBOX_TRANSPORT=rpc"},{"type":"text","value":" for the Sandbox DO). The transport must match on every\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" for an id, so a custom provider must pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ transport: 'rpc' }"},{"type":"text","value":"\ntoo. Override to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'http'"},{"type":"text","value":" only if you don't use tunnel previews."}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"two-execution-models","text":"Two execution models","level":2},{"id":"do-drives-container-default","text":"DO-drives-container (default)","level":3},{"id":"co-located-in-container","text":"Co-located (in-container)","level":3},{"id":"durable-runs-at-the-edge","text":"Durable runs at the edge","level":2},{"id":"stall-watchdog","text":"Stall watchdog","level":3},{"id":"three-layers-three-homes","text":"Three layers, three homes","level":3},{"id":"what-edge-durability-does-not-buy-you","text":"What edge durability does not buy you","level":3},{"id":"disconnect-detaches-stop-cancels","text":"Disconnect detaches; Stop cancels","level":3},{"id":"callback-hosts-bridge-vs-preview","text":"Callback hosts: bridge vs preview","level":2},{"id":"exposing-a-live-preview","text":"Exposing a live preview","level":2},{"id":"why-a-quick-tunnel-not-exposeport","text":"Why a quick tunnel, not exposePort","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou want your coding agent to run **at the edge** — UI, agent loop, and the\nsandbox container all in one Cloudflare Worker — and to hand users a **live\npreview URL** of whatever the agent builds. `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare`\nprovides the `cloudflareSandbox` provider plus a ready-made agent coordinator so\nyou don't hand-roll the Worker ↔ container plumbing.\n\nThis page covers the edge-specific concerns. For the provider-agnostic basics\n(workspace, tools, policy, lifecycle) start at the [Overview](./overview).\n\n## Two execution models\n\nWhere the harness loop and its [tool bridge](./tools) run is a deployment\nchoice. The Cloudflare layer supports two shapes.\n\n### DO-drives-container (default)\n\nThe orchestrator (a Durable Object) runs `chat()` and the tool-bridge; the\ncontainer only runs the agent CLI. The bridge is served from the orchestrator's\nown `fetch` handler — no raw TCP listener — and the agent reaches it across the\ncontainer → orchestrator boundary, so the **whole MCP protocol** crosses that\nboundary. The `examples/sandbox-cloudflare` TanStack Start app demonstrates this:\nUI, agent, Durable Objects, and the container in one Worker.\n\n### Co-located (in-container)\n\nThe harness loop **and** the tool-bridge run inside the container — the\nin-container sandbox is just `localProcessSandbox()`, with native stdin and a\nlocalhost `node:http` bridge. The only thing that still crosses back to the\norchestrator is host **tool execution**: a `chat()` tool's `execute()` closure\n(your DB, secrets, app state) lives on the orchestrator, not in the container.\nThe public surface shrinks from the whole MCP protocol to a single authenticated\ntool-exec call.\n\nEnable it with `createCloudflareSandboxAgent({ mode: 'colocated' })` plus a\n`runInContainerHarness` container program from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/runner`. The seam is four exports from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox`: the orchestrator serializes its tools with\n`toolDescriptors(tools)` and ships the descriptors in; the container rebuilds\nthem with `remoteToolStubs(descriptors, executor)`, where each stub's `execute()`\ndelegates to a `RemoteToolExecutor` (`httpRemoteToolExecutor(url, token)` POSTs\n`{ name, args }` back); the orchestrator answers that one call with\n`executeHostTool(tools, name, args)`.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport {\n  defineSandbox,\n  defineWorkspace,\n  httpRemoteToolExecutor,\n  remoteToolStubs,\n  withSandbox,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\nimport { request } from './run-request'\n\n// Inside the container: the orchestrator POSTed `{ messages, toolDescriptors,\n// toolExecUrl, toolExecToken }`. Rebuild its tools as stubs whose execute()\n// POSTs back; the adapter bridges them over the in-container localhost MCP\n// transport, and only that one tool-exec call leaves the container.\nchat({\n  threadId: request.threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages: request.messages,\n  tools: remoteToolStubs(\n    request.toolDescriptors,\n    httpRemoteToolExecutor(request.toolExecUrl, request.toolExecToken),\n  ),\n  // The in-container sandbox is just local-process (native stdin + a localhost\n  // node:http bridge).\n  middleware: [\n    withSandbox(\n      defineSandbox({\n        id: 'in-container',\n        provider: localProcessSandbox(),\n        workspace: defineWorkspace({ source: { type: 'none' } }),\n      }),\n    ),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n## Durable runs at the edge\n\nCloudflare is the **log-first tier** of the portable durable-runs protocol —\nnot a separate architecture. Read\n[Durable Runs Explained](./durable-runs#the-two-tiers) first; this section is\nwhat the edge changes and what it deliberately does not.\n\nThe coordinator Durable Object owns each run and persists every emitted chunk\ninto a DO-storage-backed run log (`DurableObjectRunEventLog`) under a monotonic\n`seq`. Clients only ever tail that log from a cursor, so a refresh, a dropped\nWebSocket, or a coordinator that hibernated between chunks reconnects **without\never touching the sandbox**. The [journal](./journal) keeps its portable job on\nthe driver side — warm reattach and the driver's resume position — and the\nprecedence rule is the portable one: the log wins for what clients see, the\njournal wins for where the driver resumes.\n\nWhat is genuinely Cloudflare-specific is the DO's *supervised lifetime*: the\ndriver outlives any request and `alarm()` gives it a scheduler. Off Cloudflare,\n[takeover](./takeover) is the substitute for exactly that, which is why the\njournal stays mandatory on every tier.\n\n\"One portable protocol\" is literal here, not a framing: the coordinator drives\nruns with **core's run driver** (`RunController` from `@tanstack/ai-sandbox`),\nbound to the DO log by two adapters — `runLogStore` exposes the log as core's\n`RunStore`, and `runLogStream` exposes one run of it as core's\n`StreamDurability`. Both are exported from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent` for apps composing directly, and they\nare what lets the portable pieces (`alignToStoredLog`, `replayRunStream`) work\nagainst the DO log the same way they do against `memoryStream` or\n`durableStream`.\n\nThe vocabulary is core's throughout: statuses are `completed` / `failed` /\n`aborted`, and the record is core's `RunRecord` plus the log's own `lastSeq`\ncursor (`RunLogRecord`). Records a Durable Object persisted under the pre-1.0\nlayout (`done` / `error` statuses, `createdAt`/`updatedAt` fields) are\nmigrated in place on first read — nothing to run, but note that\n`GET /runs/:id` and the WebSocket terminal `status` frame now carry the\nconverged status strings and field names.\n\n### Stall watchdog\n\n`createCloudflareSandboxAgent` sets one stall policy for the whole app. The\n`stallTimeoutMs` field applies to every run in both `do-drives` (the default)\nand `colocated` mode:\n\n| Value | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| Omitted | Treat the run as stalled after `300000` ms (five minutes) without persisted activity |\n| Positive safe integer | Use that many milliseconds as the stall threshold |\n| `false` | Disable stall detection |\n\n```ts\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { createCloudflareSandboxAgent } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\n\nexport const agent = createCloudflareSandboxAgent({\n  adapter: () => grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  stallTimeoutMs: 10 * 60_000,\n})\n```\n\nThe alarm checks every 30 seconds, or every `stallTimeoutMs` when that is\nshorter, so detection can lag the configured threshold by up to one check\ninterval. Persisted run events refresh\n`updatedAt`; authenticated `/_bridge` and `/tool-exec` callbacks also refresh it\non arrival and completion. Unknown-run and unauthorized requests do not.\n\nChoose a timeout longer than the longest legitimate quiet period. Native\noperations that emit no event and make no callback are invisible to the\nwatchdog. An authenticated callback is protected while it remains in flight.\nA callback that never completes can leave an orphaned `running` record.\nDisabling the watchdog with `false` can do the same. When a stall is detected, the watchdog marks\nthe run log `failed`; it may not kill the underlying agent process or container.\nUse provider lifecycle controls and [reaping](./reaping) for resource cleanup.\n\n### Three layers, three homes\n\nKeep these separate — each has a different home on Cloudflare, and conflating\nthem is how workspace bytes end up \"durably\" in `/tmp`:\n\n| Layer | Where it lives on Cloudflare | Covered in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Run + events | The coordinator DO's run log (DO storage) | [The Run Journal](./journal), [Takeover](./takeover) |\n| Workspace (the files the agent edits) | The container filesystem — **not durable**; re-provision it on create | [Workspace](./workspace), [Provisioning](./provisioning) |\n| Artifacts (outputs worth keeping) | R2, via a persistence `BlobStore` / `ArtifactStore` | [Keep generated files](../persistence/keep-generated-files) |\n\n### What edge durability does not buy you\n\n- **The journal is not durable here.** `cloudflareSandbox` declares\n  `durableFilesystem: false`: the `/tmp` journal lives exactly as long as the\n  container instance. That is fine — on this tier the DO log is the durable\n  copy and the journal is driver recovery — but it means the journal must never\n  be treated as the store of record.\n- **Refresh safety is not sleep survival.** The log makes *reconnecting\n  clients* safe. It does not keep the container awake: if the container stops,\n  the agent process dies mid-run like anywhere else, and what the log preserves\n  is everything emitted up to that point.\n- **The `sleepAfter` footgun.** The Sandbox container class carries a\n  `sleepAfter` idle timeout. A run that is *detached but quiet* — nobody\n  connected, the agent thinking or waiting — can look idle, and an idle\n  container sleeps. The symptom is a run that \"died for no reason\" while the\n  log dutifully preserved its prefix, which reads as durability failing when it\n  is the container lifecycle working as configured. Size `sleepAfter` well\n  above your longest expected quiet stretch before relying on detached runs.\n- **Name the sandbox by `threadId`.** Prefer\n  `defineSandbox({ id: input.threadId, … })` (as `examples/sandbox-cloudflare`\n  does) over a fixed id: reconnects, `exposePreview`, and `reuse: 'thread'`\n  then all address the same container even across DO eviction.\n\n### Disconnect detaches; Stop cancels\n\nThe portable rule holds on this path: a closed tab or dropped WebSocket only\ndetaches the tail — the DO keeps driving and the log keeps filling. Stopping is\nan explicit cancel, and on this provider that has teeth: `killableProcesses` is\n`false` (no signal crosses the Workers RPC boundary), so the only cancel that\nactually stops the agent is destroying the container. Aborting the run so the\nlog is marked `aborted`, without the destroy, leaves the agent running — and\nbilling — behind a UI that says stopped. See\n[what cancel means on a provider that cannot kill](./takeover#what-cancel-means-on-a-provider-that-cannot-kill).\n\n## Callback hosts: bridge vs preview\n\nIn both models the container is **off-isolate compute** — it can't use a service\nbinding or an in-process call to reach the Worker, only the network. So the\ncontainer's callback URLs need real hosts. There are **two distinct surfaces**\nwith different reachers and therefore different correct values, resolved by\n`resolveBridgeOrigin` and `resolvePreviewHost` (both from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent`).\n\n- **Bridge / tool-exec** (container → Worker: `/_bridge`, `/tool-exec`). Just\n  needs to *reach* the Worker. `PUBLIC_HOSTNAME` is optional — when unset, the\n  host is derived from the `POST /runs` trigger request, so a `*.workers.dev`\n  deploy works with **zero config**, and **local dev uses\n  `host.docker.internal`** (the Docker host gateway, over `http`) — no tunnel.\n\n  > Request-derivation is safe **on Cloudflare**, where it would be unsafe on a\n  > generic Node server: the edge dispatches a request to your Worker only when\n  > its hostname matches a route you own, so the request `Host` is always one of\n  > your own hostnames — never attacker-chosen — and the per-run bearer token\n  > that rides the URL can't be steered off-domain. On plain Node the `Host`\n  > header is attacker-controlled, which is why request-derivation there would be\n  > a token-exfil / SSRF vector. (See [Tools](./tools) for the non-edge bridge.)\n\n- **Preview** (browser → Worker → container: `exposePort`). Needs **wildcard\n  DNS**, so `PREVIEW_HOSTNAME` is a *separate* knob. **Local** uses `*.localhost`\n  (browsers resolve it to loopback with zero setup — previews work locally with\n  no tunnel). **Deployed** needs a **custom domain** with a `*.<domain>` route:\n  `*.workers.dev` has no wildcard subdomains, so the SDK's `exposePort` rejects\n  it and `resolvePreviewHost` throws a clear error pointing at `PREVIEW_HOSTNAME`\n  instead of failing deep in a run.\n\n## Exposing a live preview\n\nThe package ships the browser-preview wiring so you don't hand-roll it — both\nexported from `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent`:\n\n- **`exposePreviewTool(input, env)`** — a ready-made `chat()` server tool (the\n  agent sees it as `exposePreview`). It addresses the run's container by\n  `threadId` and opens a **Cloudflare quick tunnel** to the dev server's port\n  (`sandbox.tunnels.get(port)`), returning a\n  `https://<name>.trycloudflare.com` URL.\n- **`PREVIEW_GUIDANCE`** — a system prompt that tells the agent how to start a\n  dev server whose tunnel preview works. App-agnostic on purpose.\n\nThe factory is **harness-agnostic about auth**: it binds no API key of its own.\nYour app declares the key its harness needs (`XAI_API_KEY` for Grok Build,\n`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for Claude Code, `CODEX_API_KEY` for Codex, …) on its own env\ntype and supplies it as a [workspace secret](./provisioning) — the coordinator\ninjects each declared secret into the sandbox env by name.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  PREVIEW_GUIDANCE,\n  createCloudflareSandboxAgent,\n  exposePreviewTool,\n  resolvePreviewHost,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\nimport { cloudflareSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare'\nimport { createSecrets, defineSandbox, defineWorkspace } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport type { SandboxAgentEnv } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\n\n// Extend the package's harness-agnostic env with the key YOUR harness needs.\ninterface AppEnv extends SandboxAgentEnv {\n  XAI_API_KEY: string\n}\n\nexport const agent = createCloudflareSandboxAgent<AppEnv>({\n  adapter: () => grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  systemPrompts: [PREVIEW_GUIDANCE],\n  tools: (input, env) => [exposePreviewTool(input, env)],\n  // Supply the harness's auth here — the package binds no key. The `sandbox`\n  // resolver receives the Worker `env` per run, so the secret VALUE is read from\n  // it. A different harness declares its own, e.g. `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for Claude\n  // Code or `CODEX_API_KEY` for Codex.\n  sandbox: (input, env) =>\n    defineSandbox({\n      id: 'cf-edge-agent',\n      provider: cloudflareSandbox({\n        binding: env.Sandbox,\n        previewHostname: resolvePreviewHost(env, input),\n      }),\n      workspace: defineWorkspace({\n        source: { type: 'none' },\n        secrets: createSecrets({ XAI_API_KEY: env.XAI_API_KEY }),\n      }),\n      lifecycle: { reuse: 'thread' },\n    }),\n})\n```\n\n> The runnable example lives at\n> [`examples/sandbox-cloudflare`](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/tree/main/examples/sandbox-cloudflare):\n> one app that runs Claude Code, Codex, or Grok Build — pick the harness in the\n> UI (or via the `HARNESS` var). Same edge topology, different adapter + key.\n\n### Why a quick tunnel, not `exposePort`\n\n`exposePort` + `proxyToSandbox` routes the preview through the Worker's own\norigin. In local dev that origin is your Vite dev server, and Vite's middleware\nthen serves the preview's module/asset requests (`/@vite/client`, `/src/*`,\n`/@fs/*`) from your **host** instead of the container — the page loads the wrong\ncode and breaks.\n\nA quick tunnel is served by `cloudflared` **inside** the sandbox (`cloudflared`\nships in the `cloudflare/sandbox` base image), so it bypasses the Vite port\nentirely, needs **no custom domain** on a deploy, and forwards WebSockets — so\nthe app's HMR works. The one requirement, which `PREVIEW_GUIDANCE` instructs, is\nthat the dev server **accept the tunnel hostname** (servers reject unknown\nhosts): Vite `server: { host: true, allowedHosts: true }`, webpack-dev-server\n`allowedHosts: 'all'`. (`exposePort` + `resolvePreviewHost` remain available for\napps that want the Worker to front the request on a custom domain.)\n\n> **Transport:** `sandbox.tunnels` exists only on the SDK's **RPC** transport —\n> on the default `http` it throws *\"requires the RPC transport\"*. So\n> `cloudflareSandbox` defaults to `transport: 'rpc'` (and the example also sets\n> `SANDBOX_TRANSPORT=rpc` for the Sandbox DO). The transport must match on every\n> `getSandbox()` for an id, so a custom provider must pass `{ transport: 'rpc' }`\n> too. Override to `'http'` only if you don't use tunnel previews.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Cloudflare (Edge, Advanced)\nid: cloudflare\norder: 11\ndescription: \"Run a coding-agent harness and a live preview at the edge with @tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare — Workers, Containers, and Durable Objects in one deploy.\"\n---\n\nYou want your coding agent to run **at the edge** — UI, agent loop, and the\nsandbox container all in one Cloudflare Worker — and to hand users a **live\npreview URL** of whatever the agent builds. `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare`\nprovides the `cloudflareSandbox` provider plus a ready-made agent coordinator so\nyou don't hand-roll the Worker ↔ container plumbing.\n\nThis page covers the edge-specific concerns. For the provider-agnostic basics\n(workspace, tools, policy, lifecycle) start at the [Overview](./overview).\n\n## Two execution models\n\nWhere the harness loop and its [tool bridge](./tools) run is a deployment\nchoice. The Cloudflare layer supports two shapes.\n\n### DO-drives-container (default)\n\nThe orchestrator (a Durable Object) runs `chat()` and the tool-bridge; the\ncontainer only runs the agent CLI. The bridge is served from the orchestrator's\nown `fetch` handler — no raw TCP listener — and the agent reaches it across the\ncontainer → orchestrator boundary, so the **whole MCP protocol** crosses that\nboundary. The `examples/sandbox-cloudflare` TanStack Start app demonstrates this:\nUI, agent, Durable Objects, and the container in one Worker.\n\n### Co-located (in-container)\n\nThe harness loop **and** the tool-bridge run inside the container — the\nin-container sandbox is just `localProcessSandbox()`, with native stdin and a\nlocalhost `node:http` bridge. The only thing that still crosses back to the\norchestrator is host **tool execution**: a `chat()` tool's `execute()` closure\n(your DB, secrets, app state) lives on the orchestrator, not in the container.\nThe public surface shrinks from the whole MCP protocol to a single authenticated\ntool-exec call.\n\nEnable it with `createCloudflareSandboxAgent({ mode: 'colocated' })` plus a\n`runInContainerHarness` container program from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/runner`. The seam is four exports from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox`: the orchestrator serializes its tools with\n`toolDescriptors(tools)` and ships the descriptors in; the container rebuilds\nthem with `remoteToolStubs(descriptors, executor)`, where each stub's `execute()`\ndelegates to a `RemoteToolExecutor` (`httpRemoteToolExecutor(url, token)` POSTs\n`{ name, args }` back); the orchestrator answers that one call with\n`executeHostTool(tools, name, args)`.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport {\n  defineSandbox,\n  defineWorkspace,\n  httpRemoteToolExecutor,\n  remoteToolStubs,\n  withSandbox,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\nimport { request } from './run-request'\n\n// Inside the container: the orchestrator POSTed `{ messages, toolDescriptors,\n// toolExecUrl, toolExecToken }`. Rebuild its tools as stubs whose execute()\n// POSTs back; the adapter bridges them over the in-container localhost MCP\n// transport, and only that one tool-exec call leaves the container.\nchat({\n  threadId: request.threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages: request.messages,\n  tools: remoteToolStubs(\n    request.toolDescriptors,\n    httpRemoteToolExecutor(request.toolExecUrl, request.toolExecToken),\n  ),\n  // The in-container sandbox is just local-process (native stdin + a localhost\n  // node:http bridge).\n  middleware: [\n    withSandbox(\n      defineSandbox({\n        id: 'in-container',\n        provider: localProcessSandbox(),\n        workspace: defineWorkspace({ source: { type: 'none' } }),\n      }),\n    ),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n## Durable runs at the edge\n\nCloudflare is the **log-first tier** of the portable durable-runs protocol —\nnot a separate architecture. Read\n[Durable Runs Explained](./durable-runs#the-two-tiers) first; this section is\nwhat the edge changes and what it deliberately does not.\n\nThe coordinator Durable Object owns each run and persists every emitted chunk\ninto a DO-storage-backed run log (`DurableObjectRunEventLog`) under a monotonic\n`seq`. Clients only ever tail that log from a cursor, so a refresh, a dropped\nWebSocket, or a coordinator that hibernated between chunks reconnects **without\never touching the sandbox**. The [journal](./journal) keeps its portable job on\nthe driver side — warm reattach and the driver's resume position — and the\nprecedence rule is the portable one: the log wins for what clients see, the\njournal wins for where the driver resumes.\n\nWhat is genuinely Cloudflare-specific is the DO's *supervised lifetime*: the\ndriver outlives any request and `alarm()` gives it a scheduler. Off Cloudflare,\n[takeover](./takeover) is the substitute for exactly that, which is why the\njournal stays mandatory on every tier.\n\n\"One portable protocol\" is literal here, not a framing: the coordinator drives\nruns with **core's run driver** (`RunController` from `@tanstack/ai-sandbox`),\nbound to the DO log by two adapters — `runLogStore` exposes the log as core's\n`RunStore`, and `runLogStream` exposes one run of it as core's\n`StreamDurability`. Both are exported from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent` for apps composing directly, and they\nare what lets the portable pieces (`alignToStoredLog`, `replayRunStream`) work\nagainst the DO log the same way they do against `memoryStream` or\n`durableStream`.\n\nThe vocabulary is core's throughout: statuses are `completed` / `failed` /\n`aborted`, and the record is core's `RunRecord` plus the log's own `lastSeq`\ncursor (`RunLogRecord`). Records a Durable Object persisted under the pre-1.0\nlayout (`done` / `error` statuses, `createdAt`/`updatedAt` fields) are\nmigrated in place on first read — nothing to run, but note that\n`GET /runs/:id` and the WebSocket terminal `status` frame now carry the\nconverged status strings and field names.\n\n### Stall watchdog\n\n`createCloudflareSandboxAgent` sets one stall policy for the whole app. The\n`stallTimeoutMs` field applies to every run in both `do-drives` (the default)\nand `colocated` mode:\n\n| Value | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| Omitted | Treat the run as stalled after `300000` ms (five minutes) without persisted activity |\n| Positive safe integer | Use that many milliseconds as the stall threshold |\n| `false` | Disable stall detection |\n\n```ts\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { createCloudflareSandboxAgent } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\n\nexport const agent = createCloudflareSandboxAgent({\n  adapter: () => grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  stallTimeoutMs: 10 * 60_000,\n})\n```\n\nThe alarm checks every 30 seconds, or every `stallTimeoutMs` when that is\nshorter, so detection can lag the configured threshold by up to one check\ninterval. Persisted run events refresh\n`updatedAt`; authenticated `/_bridge` and `/tool-exec` callbacks also refresh it\non arrival and completion. Unknown-run and unauthorized requests do not.\n\nChoose a timeout longer than the longest legitimate quiet period. Native\noperations that emit no event and make no callback are invisible to the\nwatchdog. An authenticated callback is protected while it remains in flight.\nA callback that never completes can leave an orphaned `running` record.\nDisabling the watchdog with `false` can do the same. When a stall is detected, the watchdog marks\nthe run log `failed`; it may not kill the underlying agent process or container.\nUse provider lifecycle controls and [reaping](./reaping) for resource cleanup.\n\n### Three layers, three homes\n\nKeep these separate — each has a different home on Cloudflare, and conflating\nthem is how workspace bytes end up \"durably\" in `/tmp`:\n\n| Layer | Where it lives on Cloudflare | Covered in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Run + events | The coordinator DO's run log (DO storage) | [The Run Journal](./journal), [Takeover](./takeover) |\n| Workspace (the files the agent edits) | The container filesystem — **not durable**; re-provision it on create | [Workspace](./workspace), [Provisioning](./provisioning) |\n| Artifacts (outputs worth keeping) | R2, via a persistence `BlobStore` / `ArtifactStore` | [Keep generated files](../persistence/keep-generated-files) |\n\n### What edge durability does not buy you\n\n- **The journal is not durable here.** `cloudflareSandbox` declares\n  `durableFilesystem: false`: the `/tmp` journal lives exactly as long as the\n  container instance. That is fine — on this tier the DO log is the durable\n  copy and the journal is driver recovery — but it means the journal must never\n  be treated as the store of record.\n- **Refresh safety is not sleep survival.** The log makes *reconnecting\n  clients* safe. It does not keep the container awake: if the container stops,\n  the agent process dies mid-run like anywhere else, and what the log preserves\n  is everything emitted up to that point.\n- **The `sleepAfter` footgun.** The Sandbox container class carries a\n  `sleepAfter` idle timeout. A run that is *detached but quiet* — nobody\n  connected, the agent thinking or waiting — can look idle, and an idle\n  container sleeps. The symptom is a run that \"died for no reason\" while the\n  log dutifully preserved its prefix, which reads as durability failing when it\n  is the container lifecycle working as configured. Size `sleepAfter` well\n  above your longest expected quiet stretch before relying on detached runs.\n- **Name the sandbox by `threadId`.** Prefer\n  `defineSandbox({ id: input.threadId, … })` (as `examples/sandbox-cloudflare`\n  does) over a fixed id: reconnects, `exposePreview`, and `reuse: 'thread'`\n  then all address the same container even across DO eviction.\n\n### Disconnect detaches; Stop cancels\n\nThe portable rule holds on this path: a closed tab or dropped WebSocket only\ndetaches the tail — the DO keeps driving and the log keeps filling. Stopping is\nan explicit cancel, and on this provider that has teeth: `killableProcesses` is\n`false` (no signal crosses the Workers RPC boundary), so the only cancel that\nactually stops the agent is destroying the container. Aborting the run so the\nlog is marked `aborted`, without the destroy, leaves the agent running — and\nbilling — behind a UI that says stopped. See\n[what cancel means on a provider that cannot kill](./takeover#what-cancel-means-on-a-provider-that-cannot-kill).\n\n## Callback hosts: bridge vs preview\n\nIn both models the container is **off-isolate compute** — it can't use a service\nbinding or an in-process call to reach the Worker, only the network. So the\ncontainer's callback URLs need real hosts. There are **two distinct surfaces**\nwith different reachers and therefore different correct values, resolved by\n`resolveBridgeOrigin` and `resolvePreviewHost` (both from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent`).\n\n- **Bridge / tool-exec** (container → Worker: `/_bridge`, `/tool-exec`). Just\n  needs to *reach* the Worker. `PUBLIC_HOSTNAME` is optional — when unset, the\n  host is derived from the `POST /runs` trigger request, so a `*.workers.dev`\n  deploy works with **zero config**, and **local dev uses\n  `host.docker.internal`** (the Docker host gateway, over `http`) — no tunnel.\n\n  > Request-derivation is safe **on Cloudflare**, where it would be unsafe on a\n  > generic Node server: the edge dispatches a request to your Worker only when\n  > its hostname matches a route you own, so the request `Host` is always one of\n  > your own hostnames — never attacker-chosen — and the per-run bearer token\n  > that rides the URL can't be steered off-domain. On plain Node the `Host`\n  > header is attacker-controlled, which is why request-derivation there would be\n  > a token-exfil / SSRF vector. (See [Tools](./tools) for the non-edge bridge.)\n\n- **Preview** (browser → Worker → container: `exposePort`). Needs **wildcard\n  DNS**, so `PREVIEW_HOSTNAME` is a *separate* knob. **Local** uses `*.localhost`\n  (browsers resolve it to loopback with zero setup — previews work locally with\n  no tunnel). **Deployed** needs a **custom domain** with a `*.<domain>` route:\n  `*.workers.dev` has no wildcard subdomains, so the SDK's `exposePort` rejects\n  it and `resolvePreviewHost` throws a clear error pointing at `PREVIEW_HOSTNAME`\n  instead of failing deep in a run.\n\n## Exposing a live preview\n\nThe package ships the browser-preview wiring so you don't hand-roll it — both\nexported from `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent`:\n\n- **`exposePreviewTool(input, env)`** — a ready-made `chat()` server tool (the\n  agent sees it as `exposePreview`). It addresses the run's container by\n  `threadId` and opens a **Cloudflare quick tunnel** to the dev server's port\n  (`sandbox.tunnels.get(port)`), returning a\n  `https://<name>.trycloudflare.com` URL.\n- **`PREVIEW_GUIDANCE`** — a system prompt that tells the agent how to start a\n  dev server whose tunnel preview works. App-agnostic on purpose.\n\nThe factory is **harness-agnostic about auth**: it binds no API key of its own.\nYour app declares the key its harness needs (`XAI_API_KEY` for Grok Build,\n`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for Claude Code, `CODEX_API_KEY` for Codex, …) on its own env\ntype and supplies it as a [workspace secret](./provisioning) — the coordinator\ninjects each declared secret into the sandbox env by name.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  PREVIEW_GUIDANCE,\n  createCloudflareSandboxAgent,\n  exposePreviewTool,\n  resolvePreviewHost,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\nimport { cloudflareSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare'\nimport { createSecrets, defineSandbox, defineWorkspace } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport type { SandboxAgentEnv } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-cloudflare/agent'\n\n// Extend the package's harness-agnostic env with the key YOUR harness needs.\ninterface AppEnv extends SandboxAgentEnv {\n  XAI_API_KEY: string\n}\n\nexport const agent = createCloudflareSandboxAgent<AppEnv>({\n  adapter: () => grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  systemPrompts: [PREVIEW_GUIDANCE],\n  tools: (input, env) => [exposePreviewTool(input, env)],\n  // Supply the harness's auth here — the package binds no key. The `sandbox`\n  // resolver receives the Worker `env` per run, so the secret VALUE is read from\n  // it. A different harness declares its own, e.g. `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for Claude\n  // Code or `CODEX_API_KEY` for Codex.\n  sandbox: (input, env) =>\n    defineSandbox({\n      id: 'cf-edge-agent',\n      provider: cloudflareSandbox({\n        binding: env.Sandbox,\n        previewHostname: resolvePreviewHost(env, input),\n      }),\n      workspace: defineWorkspace({\n        source: { type: 'none' },\n        secrets: createSecrets({ XAI_API_KEY: env.XAI_API_KEY }),\n      }),\n      lifecycle: { reuse: 'thread' },\n    }),\n})\n```\n\n> The runnable example lives at\n> [`examples/sandbox-cloudflare`](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/tree/main/examples/sandbox-cloudflare):\n> one app that runs Claude Code, Codex, or Grok Build — pick the harness in the\n> UI (or via the `HARNESS` var). Same edge topology, different adapter + key.\n\n### Why a quick tunnel, not `exposePort`\n\n`exposePort` + `proxyToSandbox` routes the preview through the Worker's own\norigin. In local dev that origin is your Vite dev server, and Vite's middleware\nthen serves the preview's module/asset requests (`/@vite/client`, `/src/*`,\n`/@fs/*`) from your **host** instead of the container — the page loads the wrong\ncode and breaks.\n\nA quick tunnel is served by `cloudflared` **inside** the sandbox (`cloudflared`\nships in the `cloudflare/sandbox` base image), so it bypasses the Vite port\nentirely, needs **no custom domain** on a deploy, and forwards WebSockets — so\nthe app's HMR works. The one requirement, which `PREVIEW_GUIDANCE` instructs, is\nthat the dev server **accept the tunnel hostname** (servers reject unknown\nhosts): Vite `server: { host: true, allowedHosts: true }`, webpack-dev-server\n`allowedHosts: 'all'`. (`exposePort` + `resolvePreviewHost` remain available for\napps that want the Worker to front the request on a custom domain.)\n\n> **Transport:** `sandbox.tunnels` exists only on the SDK's **RPC** transport —\n> on the default `http` it throws *\"requires the RPC transport\"*. So\n> `cloudflareSandbox` defaults to `transport: 'rpc'` (and the example also sets\n> `SANDBOX_TRANSPORT=rpc` for the Sandbox DO). The transport must match on every\n> `getSandbox()` for an id, so a custom provider must pass `{ transport: 'rpc' }`\n> too. Override to `'http'` only if you don't use tunnel previews.\n"}
